# Part 3: Inside sibl

This part goes inside the source tree. It maps the modules, follows one input file through the pipeline, and explains the test and contribution rules.

## Source map

| File | Phase | Role |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `src/alloc.zig` | 1.2 | Arena and scratch allocators |
| `src/args.zig` | 1.3 | CLI argument parser |
| `src/target.zig` | 1.4 | Target detection and page size |
| `src/file.zig` | 1.5 | Memory-mapped file reader |
| `src/elf.zig` | 2 | ELF parsing |
| `src/strtab.zig` | 2.4 | String table reader and builder |
| `src/coff.zig` | 3 | PE and COFF parsing |
| `src/macho.zig` | 4 | Mach-O parsing |
| `src/archive.zig` | 5 | Static archive parsing |
| `src/symbol.zig` | 6 | Global symbol table and resolution |
| `src/layout.zig` | 7 | Section grouping, segments, size accounting |
| `src/reloc.zig` | 8 | Relocation engine |
| `src/entry.zig` | 9.1 | Entry point resolution |
| `src/section_rules.zig` | 9.2 | Placement rules and memory regions |
| `src/defsym.zig` | 9.3 | `--defsym` symbol assignment |
| `src/templates.zig` | 9.4 | Default layout templates |
| `src/root.zig` | all | Public re-exports |
| `src/main.zig` | all | Linker executable entry point |

## Design rules

sibl follows a few simple rules.

- Allocations are explicit. Pass an allocator instead of using hidden global state.
- Parsing is zero-copy. Parsers return slices into the mapped input file.
- Bulk data uses an arena. The arena frees everything in one call at the end.
- Errors are returned, not hidden. Callers decide how to handle them.
- Modules are small and focused. Each module owns one stage.

## One file through the pipeline

Take one ELF object file. The pipeline looks like this.

1. `main.zig` reads the arguments and detects the target.
2. `file.zig` maps the input file into memory.
3. `elf.zig` parses the header, section headers, symbols, and relocations.
4. `symbol.zig` adds the ELF symbols to the global symbol table.
5. `layout.zig` adds allocatable sections to the layout.
6. Placement rules, `--defsym`, and the template adjust the plan.
7. `layout.computeAddresses` assigns virtual addresses and file offsets.
8. `layout.buildSegments` groups sections into segments.
9. `layout.accountSizes` finalizes sizes.
10. `entry.zig` resolves the entry point.
11. `reloc.zig` can apply relocations when a buffer is available.

The current executable stops after printing the plan. Binary emission is Phase 13.

## Memory flow

- The arena allocator owns symbols, section lists, and parsed data.
- The mapped file owns the raw bytes. It stays alive until parsing is done.
- Output section names are heap-owned and freed by `OutputSection.deinit`.
- Size reports own their segment arrays and are freed with `freeSizeReport`.

## Tests

Tests live next to the code in each module. Run them with:

```sh
zig build test
```

When you add a module, add a `test` block in `root.zig` so the test runner picks it up.

Good tests use real formulas and real inputs. Avoid stubs. Test both success and error paths.

## Adding a new module

1. Create `src/new_module.zig`.
2. Add a module-level comment with the phase and contract.
3. Document every public type, function, and error.
4. Add unit tests next to the code.
5. Re-export public types from `src/root.zig`.
6. Add the module to the test runner in `root.zig`.
7. Update `ROADMAP.md`.

## Contribution checklist

- Keep the zero-copy and arena style.
- Keep functions small and focused.
- Return errors instead of panicking.
- Run `zig build test` before sending a change.
- Keep documentation in sync with code.

